<p>A relational schema describes what the application knows, which facts belong together and which states it will refuse to store. It includes the tables and columns, but also their meanings: whether two rows are duplicate bookings, whether a price is today’s offer or yesterday’s agreement, and what should remain when a customer leaves.</p>
<p>Those decisions are easiest to test while the design is small. We will build a booking service for organisations running classes. Mina can book a place on a scheduled session, change her contact details and cancel. An organisation needs a roster, a count of occupied places and a record of what it agreed to charge. The example uses PostgreSQL 18; the reasoning starts from the application’s promises.</p>
<p>By the end, we will have a schema, queries with expected answers, changes that must fail and a short list of obligations the schema does not enforce. Read through that first design before choosing the deeper articles on values, constraints, normalisation or history.</p>

